# One line-ending convention everywhere. CI regenerates docs/STATUS.md on Windows, Linux and
# macOS and fails the build on any diff, so a CRLF/LF flip would rewrite every line and break
# that check on two legs out of three.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary and generated content that must not be touched.
*.png binary
*.snk binary
*.trx text eol=lf

# JavaScript is byte-for-byte content here, not text to normalise. Below the `* text=auto` line on
# purpose: the LAST matching pattern wins, so this has to come after it to take effect.
#
# Microsoft's reason, verbatim (learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/host-and-deploy/webassembly/
# github-pages, fetched 2026-09-16): "Git treats JavaScript (JS) files, such as
# blazor.webassembly.js, as text and converts line endings from CRLF to LF in the deployment
# pipeline. These changes to JS files produce different file hashes than Blazor sends to the client.
# The mismatches result in integrity check failures on the client."
#
# What it protects HERE is narrower than that quotation, and saying so is the point: their scenario
# commits the published assets to a branch, whereas .github/workflows/pages.yml publishes in CI and
# uploads the output directly, so no framework JavaScript ever passes through Git. This rule covers
# the one piece of JavaScript this repository both commits and ships to a browser:
# demo/FuzzyRegex.Demo.Wasm/wwwroot/worker.js. Since S71 bundled the front end, the page's modules
# are TypeScript under demo/web/src and the bundle Vite builds from them is generated, gitignored
# and hashed at build time, so it never passes through Git at all. The rule deliberately does not
# extend to demo/web/scripts/clean.mjs, the only other committed module: it runs on the build
# machine, is never fetched with an integrity attribute, and keeping it text keeps its diffs
# readable.
*.js binary

# The upstream submodule is vendored read-only; its diffs are not ours to review.
upstream/** linguist-vendored
